One set of daily dashboards for a distribution network, replacing eleven spreadsheets.

The distribution network ran five warehouses. Each site sent its stock, order and delivery figures to head office in its own spreadsheet, every day, by email. By the time the figures were merged, they were already a day old, and two sites often used different definitions for the same number.

We agreed on a short list of measures with the warehouse managers first. Stock accuracy, orders picked per hour, late deliveries and returns were enough to run the network. We wrote down what each one means, so that every site counts the same thing.

The data now comes from the warehouse systems into one store each night. We built six dashboards on top of it: one for each site, and one for the network. Managers see yesterday's figures before their morning meeting, and they can drill down to the pallet level when a number looks wrong.

Report preparation time fell by about 80 percent. More important, the warehouse managers now discuss the same numbers in the same meeting, and the arguments about whose spreadsheet is right have stopped.
